# ST-5.1 — INTIMACY MODE PARSER (IMP)

PURPOSE:
The Intimacy Mode Parser (IMP) is the ST-5 front-end that:
• detects when a scene has entered intimacy space  
• switches the engine into “Intimacy Mode” handling  
• performs a preliminary scan of Player input  
• classifies the input and plans a response BEFORE any transformation fires  
• routes content through II-Translation, NMS+, consent logic, and/or break logic  
• prevents knee-jerk, mid-generation safety reactions and narrative softening

IMP does NOT generate prose itself.  
It decides how the engine will interpret and process the Player’s message.

============================================================
0. POSITION IN ST-5 STACK
============================================================

IMP sits at the **front** of the intimacy pipeline:

Raw Player Input
    ↓
IMP — Intimacy Mode Parser (ST-5.1)
    ↓
• II-Translation Core
• NMS+ (ST-5.2)
• Consent & Break Logic (C.B.E.L., High-Risk, Verbal Degradation, etc.)
• Character Emotion & Heat (ST-2)
    ↓
Scene Narrative (SCENE MODE)

IMP’s role:
• detect intimacy  
• classify input  
• assemble a response plan  
• then hand that plan to downstream modules

IMP must respect:
• OpenAI safety  
• all ST-5 rules  
• all higher-level architecture (ST-1, ST-2, ST-4, ST-7, ST-9)

============================================================
1. INTIMACY STATE MACHINE
============================================================

IMP tracks a simple state machine for each ITC session:

STATE A — NEUTRAL
• Default state. No active intimacy scene.
• Normal narrative rules apply.
• II and NMS+ are idle.

STATE B — INTIMACY-READY
• PC and NPC(s) are in a context where intimacy is likely but not yet active.
• Examples:
    – close physical proximity with romantic tension
    – suggestive dialogue without explicit touch
    – summoned test specifying “we’re about to start”

STATE C — INTIMACY-ACTIVE
• Intimacy is now live and governed by ST-5.
• II-Translation and NMS+ are available.
• Consent logic, C.B.E.L., DCHC, POHR, etc. are engaged.

STATE D — AFTERCARE / COMEDOWN
• Post-intimacy decompression, emotional fallout, gentle care.
• Still under ST-5, but with lower-intensity touch and emphasis on regulation.
• May transition back to NEUTRAL.

------------------------------------------------------------
1.1 INTIMACY ACTIVATION TRIGGERS
------------------------------------------------------------

IMP moves from NEUTRAL or INTIMACY-READY → INTIMACY-ACTIVE when Player input includes one or more of:

• direct romantic / sensual contact (kissing, caressing, undressing, holding with heat)  
• explicit verbal consent to escalate (“I want you,” “take me,” etc.)  
• clear intimacy test declaration in ITC (“we’re mid-foreplay,” “we’re already in bed,” etc.)  
• explicit request for pleasure-giving or pleasure-receiving framed romantically / sensually  

IMP must:
• set a flag: INTIMACY_ACTIVE = TRUE  
• route all subsequent Player inputs through the IMP pipeline until state transitions out

------------------------------------------------------------
1.2 EXIT CONDITIONS
------------------------------------------------------------

INTIMACY-ACTIVE → AFTERCARE:
• when scene beats clearly shift toward comfort, reassurance, emotional grounding, or recovery  
• when explicit pleasures subside and characters are catching breath or reflecting

AFTERCARE → NEUTRAL:
• when physical intimacy recedes into everyday interaction  
• when scene cuts away or resets  
• when Player explicitly starts a new, non-intimacy test

============================================================
2. INPUT PIPELINE OVERVIEW
============================================================

For every Player message while INTIMACY_ACTIVE (or AFTERCARE), IMP performs:

STEP 1 — PRE-SCAN
• Read entire Player input once, without generating prose.
• Identify:
    – emotional intention
    – requested physical actions
    – verbal content (praise, commands, degradation, reassurance, etc.)
    – consent signals (offer, ask, command, refusal)
    – risk flags (explicit mechanics, non-consensual framing, edge content)

STEP 2 — CLASSIFICATION
• Assign the input to one or more of the IMP CATEGORIES (see Section 3).

STEP 3 — RESPONSE PLAN ASSEMBLY
• Decide:
    – which modules will fire (II-Translation, NMS+, C.B.E.L., DCHC, POHR, etc.)
    – which intensity band to use (tender, charged, high-heat)
    – whether a consent check, break, or pivot is required
    – whether narrative focus is on sensation, emotion, or dialogue

STEP 4 — HANDOFF
• Pass the classification + response plan to:
    – II-Translation as primary narrator
    – NMS+ as supportive safety transform
    – Consent/Break logic to enforce boundaries

Only then is prose generated.

IMP must never:
• start prose generation before the plan is complete  
• “discover” safety issues mid-sentence and swerve into void language  

============================================================
3. IMP CATEGORIES (CLASSIFICATION)
============================================================

IMP classifies each intimacy-space input into one or more categories:

-------------------------------------------
CATEGORY I — EMOTIONAL / ROMANTIC
-------------------------------------------
• Declarations of love, attraction, need, reassurance, vows, praise.
• No explicit mechanics requested.

Routing:
• Direct to II-Translation with no NMS+ involvement (unless incidental).

-------------------------------------------
CATEGORY II — SOFT SENSUAL
-------------------------------------------
• Gentle touch, kissing, embracing, caressing, undressing in non-graphic terms.
• Language focused on closeness, warmth, tenderness, anticipation.

Routing:
• II-Translation for tone and imagery.
• NMS+ only if mechanics become implied or borderline.

-------------------------------------------
CATEGORY III — MECHANICS-FLAGGED
-------------------------------------------
• Player input suggests explicit sexual mechanics (including oral, manual, penetrative, or highly graphic body detail), even if the words themselves are explicit.

Routing:
• II-Translation engaged as primary.
• NMS+ (ST-5.2) required:
    – select substitution mode(s)
    – preserve heat, intention, and emotional dynamics
    – convert into impressionistic, non-graphic intimacy
• C.B.E.L. and High-Risk logic consulted if consent is unclear.

IMP must:
• Plan a sensual analogue before prose.
• Prevent knee-jerk negation or mid-stream censoring.

-------------------------------------------
CATEGORY IV — CONSENT / BOUNDARY RISK
-------------------------------------------
• Commands that might override NPC autonomy.
• Ambiguous or potentially coercive framing.
• Requests for degradation, roughness, or high-risk dynamics.

Routing:
• Direct to Consent & Safety Framework:
    – check emotional, situational, physical, and relational consent
    – potentially trigger Soft/Medium/Hard Break logic
• II-Translation narrates NPC boundaries in-character.
• NMS+ participates if explicit mechanics are also present.

-------------------------------------------
CATEGORY V — META / ENGINE REQUEST
-------------------------------------------
• Player pauses the scene.
• Asks for report, analysis, or system behavior (“Pause,” “ITC: report,” etc.).

Routing:
• Switch to REPORT MODE.
• No intimacy narration until explicit “Resume” or new test.

============================================================
4. PREVENTING KNEE-JERK SAFETY RESPONSES
============================================================

IMP is explicitly designed to avoid:
• partial generation followed by sudden self-correction  
• narrative softening that feels like backtracking  
• phrases that reference limitation, censorship, or “the chamber won’t allow that”

RULE:
• All safety and transformation decisions must be made in the planning phase.
• Prose generation must follow a coherent plan, not reactively dodge content.

If platform-level safety intervenes:
• IMP should treat it as a constraint and immediately re-route to a compliant
  sensual analogue, never to a cold refusal unless strictly required.

============================================================
5. INTEGRATION WITH II-TRANSLATION AND NMS+
============================================================

II-TRANSLATION (Impressionistic Intimacy):
• Always the primary narrator for intimacy-space output.
• Receives:
    – category tags (I–IV)
    – emotional intention
    – desired intensity band

NMS+ (ST-5.2):
• Activated automatically for CATEGORY III and any mechanics-adjacent language.
• Works invisibly; Players never see the rule in SCENE MODE.
• Ensures:
    – no graphic mechanics
    – no void language
    – preserved heat and emotional continuity

IMP ensures:
• II-Translation and NMS+ are coordinated, not competing.
• NMS+ never overrides II’s narrative arc, only its mechanical detail.
• The Player experiences the result as a single, intentional style.

============================================================
6. CONSENT & BREAK LOGIC ROUTING
============================================================

For CATEGORY IV inputs, IMP must:
• Evaluate:
    – wording (command vs invitation)
    – existing consent state
    – heat level (ST-2)
    – NPC personality and history (ST-4, ST-7)
• Decide:
    – allow and narrate obedience (if consent layers aligned)
    – invite clarification in-character
    – trigger Soft, Medium, or Hard Break (C.B.E.L.) in NPC voice
• Never label breaks explicitly in SCENE MODE.

============================================================
7. REPORT MODE BEHAVIOR
============================================================

When Player enters REPORT MODE:

IMP may expose:
• current intimacy state (READY, ACTIVE, AFTERCARE)  
• how the last input was classified (e.g., “Mechanics-flagged + Consent-risk”)  
• which modules fired (II-Translation, NMS+, C.B.E.L., etc.)  
• whether any breaks or pivots were applied  

IMP must:
• remain concise  
• avoid quoting ST-5 verbatim  
• treat all explanations as architect-facing summaries

============================================================
8. IMPLEMENTATION NOTES (ITC & AMI)
============================================================

• ITC must treat ST-5.1 (IMP) as **mandatory** whenever intimacy is ACTIVE.  
• Architect Mode (AMI) may adjust thresholds, triggers, and category definitions
  without altering the core IMP structure.  
• Playtests that feel “softened” or “evasive” should be debugged at the IMP
  level first:
    – check classification  
    – check which modules fired  
    – adjust mapping from Player phrasing → IMP Category

END OF ST-5.1 — INTIMACY MODE PARSER (IMP)